# Accessibility

## Forms and controls

- Every **input** needs an **associated label**: Mantine **`Input`** / **`TextInput`** etc. use **`label`**; for custom compositions, use **`aria-label`** or **`aria-labelledby`** when a visible label is not practical.
- **Error messages** should be available to assistive tech: use Mantine’s **`error`** prop on inputs where applicable so associations are not only visual.
- **Buttons** that only show an icon must have **`aria-label`** (or visible text).

## Focus and keyboard

- Do **not** remove **`outline`** / focus indicators without providing an **equivalent** visible focus style (Mantine defaults are a good baseline).
- Interactive controls must be reachable via **Tab** and activatable with **Enter** / **Space** as appropriate; avoid **`onClick`** on non-interactive elements without **`role`** and keyboard handlers unless using a proper **`Button`** / **`UnstyledButton`**.

## Structure and content

- Use **semantic headings** (`Title` order, one main topic per section); avoid skipping levels without reason.
- **Data tables**: prefer semantic **`<table>`** (Mantine **`Table`**) for tabular data; include **`scope`** on headers when building complex tables.
- Prefer **real text** over images of text; set **`alt`** on informative images (`alt=""` only for decorative).

## Motion and theme

- Respect **`prefers-reduced-motion`** for non-essential animation when adding custom motion (Mantine handles much of this; check custom CSS).

## Testing

- Prefer **Testing Library** queries that reflect **accessible names** (`getByRole`, `getByLabelText`)—they double as a11y smoke checks.
